{"qid":"Q1025","names":["Mauritania"],"title":"Mauritania","endonym":{"native":"الجمهورية الإسلامية الموريتانية","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"Mauritania, a vast and sparsely populated republic on the Atlantic coast of northwest Africa, achieved independence from France on 28 November 1960, with Moktar Ould Daddah becoming its first president in 1961. The country's territory bridges the Arab Maghreb and sub-Saharan Africa, with the Sahara covering nearly three-quarters of its land; its political economy has long been shaped by iron-ore extraction at Zouerate and, from the 1990s onward, offshore oil and gas. Ould Daddah's one-party state was overthrown in a 1978 military coup following the ruinous Western Sahara war with Polisario guerrillas, inaugurating decades of intermittent coups and transitional governments that continued into the twenty-first century. Democratic elections in 2007 and 2009 and a further military-to-civilian transition in 2019 partially stabilized governance, though the country remained listed among the world's least developed states.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is well-defined by internationally recognized borders established at independence in 1960 and confirmed by the 1975 Madrid Accords framework; no disputed frontier segments affect the core polygon.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Mauritania","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Mauritania"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Mauritania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauritania"}],"britannica":"place/mauritania","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Mauritania","rulers":[{"qid":"Q345385","name":"Moktar Ould